on of probability is assigned to favorable as opposed to unfavorable outcomes' — this would mean that favorable outcomes have higher probability. So if favorable outcome has higher probability, then it is left-skewed? That contradicts standard terminology. But per the reason's own definition: 'left-skewed if a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable as opposed to unfavorable outcomes'. This is a misstatement — typically, left-skewed means more mass on the right (higher values). But here, the reason defines left-skewed as having more probability on favorable outcomes. So under this definition, favorable outcomes are on the right, so more probability on the right → left-skewed? That is inconsistent. However, the reason says: 'a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable as opposed to unfavorable outcomes' → so if favorable outcome has higher probability, it is left-skewed. In Lottery A: 25% favorable (3000), 75% unfavorable (0) → favorable has 25% → favorable portion is 25%. In Lottery B: 20% favorable (4000), 80% unfavorable (0) → favorable portion is 20%. So A has a greater portion (25% vs 20%) of probability assigned to favorable outcomes. Therefore, under the reason's definition, A is more left-skewed than B. The reason prefers the more left-skewed lottery, so it would prefer A. However, the verbal report says the individual chose B. So the reason would prefer A, not B. But the question is whether the individual used the reason. We must assess whether the individual considered skewness as defined, with higher favorable probability meaning more left-skewed. So the information is available: favorable probability is 25% for A, 20% for B. A has more favorable probability → more left-skewed → preferred by the reason. Thus, the decision reason can be applied to the problem, and the relevant information is available.",
